# Accepted findings for the secret scanner (gitleaks).
#
# Each line is a fingerprint: commit:path:rule:line. A finding may be silenced
# only when it is a false positive, or when the value is provably dead — never
# because it is inconvenient. Verify before adding, and say here how it was
# verified.
#
# Both entries are bare variable names in .env.example, the committed
# configuration TEMPLATE, with no value behind them (`VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=`,
# `BLOB_S3_ACCESS_KEY=`). The generic-api-key rule matches the key name, not a
# secret. They are commented out at HEAD now, but gitleaks scans the whole
# history, so the original commits still need an explicit exception.
14e4b33111b118b2ef5dcc6c4e14f4195e2434fe:.env.example:generic-api-key:92

# A real VAPID private key, committed to the .env.example template in July 2026
# and therefore burned. It is not silenced because it is harmless — it is
# silenced because it is dead, and that is enforced rather than promised: its
# SHA-256 is in knownCompromisedDigests (backend/internal/config/config.go), so
# production refuses to start with it. The value cannot come back into use
# without deleting that entry.
#
# Not purged from history: rewriting every commit id to delete a key that is
# already worthless would break every link to a commit — CI artifacts, docs,
# reports — and buy nothing.
#
# Why this matters more than one finding: a scanner that is always red is a
# scanner nobody reads. This one stayed red for two weeks and hid a CRITICAL
# CVE in the published images (fixed in 3a505fc). Red must mean something new.
